==========================================
DAY 08 NOTES — Linux Security Basics
Date: 04-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- SUID = program runs with the FILE OWNER's privileges (not the user running it)
- SGID = similar, but for group; on directories, new files inherit the dir's group
- ls -la shows SUID as 's' in owner's execute slot, SGID as 's' in group's slot
- /usr/bin/passwd has SUID root — that's HOW normal users can change their own password
- SUID binaries are a classic privilege escalation target for attackers
- find -perm -4000 finds SUID files, -perm -2000 finds SGID files
- Basic hardening checklist: no unnecessary SUID, no world-writable files, 
  SSH root login disabled, monitor auth.log, minimal sudo access

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] ls -la (spotting SUID/SGID visually)
[ ] find -perm -4000
[ ] find -perm -2000
[ ] grep PermitRootLogin sshd_config

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ ls -la /usr/bin/passwd
> 

$ find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null | wc -l
> 

$ sudo grep "PermitRootLogin" /etc/ssh/sshd_config
> 


